    "Most of us treat boredom as something to escape as quickly as possible. In a waiting room or on a slow train, we reach for our phones almost without thinking. Yet people who study the feeling now tend to describe it not as an empty state but as a signal*. Like hunger or tiredness, it tells us that something in our situation needs to change.",
    "What exactly does boredom signal? In most cases, it appears when the task in front of us is either too easy or too meaningless to hold our attention. A child copying the same word twenty times and an office worker checking long lists of numbers may feel the same restlessness. {a|Their minds are not tired; they are hungry for something worth thinking about.}",
